#edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328172} {:answer/id #uuid "a11c362b-0329-5e15-9f0d-4bb67c042f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=9$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328331} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b8294a-f4a8-5a82-a733-0419b620a731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,x=0,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328625} {:answer/id #uuid "c933dda2-ec84-5062-ac4e-5f530086befd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e7e65d5-7788-5c01-8ad0-486d9b40f491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328172} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f8cee54-9108-570e-b47f-cbdac909560a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "683930f5-7231-57ef-90a9-236b0f94178e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4694" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-27}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-3)=0$ gives the solution $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3x+9=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4(1)(9) \\\\  & =9-36 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ee625a-e88b-5839-8eb5-cf2ea241dae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085777} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bc595a-a08c-502c-9e7f-3d082b50a538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085778} {:answer/id #uuid "04625b17-aa0d-540f-a752-c4761d00a508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085779} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0fc964-47c0-5489-bcac-9754e4e48f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085780} {:answer/id #uuid "71472aac-8ea6-50d8-b51b-f50282c3497c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085781}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7a417943-12c6-5645-8b1a-de1e21902255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085776}] :question/id #uuid "9804f06a-3a1e-50fe-a8f6-ab3f28486a47" :q